            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "physical_scope": "Mathematical local-existence and shell-preservation result for the explicitly displayed reduced-order vector field on flat affine Minkowski space. It asserts no global existence, physical truth, or approximation accuracy."
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-reduction-residual",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Conditional finite-interval LAD to LL residual",
        "statement": "Use the flat definitions $f_0$, $B$, and $\\Pi_U$ of [[lem-rpm-ll-reduced-ode]]. Let $I=[\\tau_-,\\tau_+]$ with $\\tau_-<\\tau_+$ be a common compact proper-time interval, and suppose each curve below is $C^3$ on an open neighborhood of $I$. For each $0<\\varepsilon\\leq\\varepsilon_*$, let $x_\\varepsilon:I\\to A$ be a curve in affine Minkowski space with $U_\\varepsilon=\\dot x_\\varepsilon$, $a_\\varepsilon=\\dot U_\\varepsilon$, and $\\eta(U_\\varepsilon,U_\\varepsilon)=-c^2$. Let $F$ be a fixed $C^2$ two-form on a neighborhood of the curves, fix a constant $\\alpha_\\varepsilon\\in\\mathbb R$ for each $\\varepsilon$, and set $f_{0,\\varepsilon}=\\alpha_\\varepsilon F(U_\\varepsilon)$ and $\\delta_\\varepsilon:=a_\\varepsilon-f_{0,\\varepsilon}$. Suppose every curve satisfies the flat LAD equation\n\n$$\na_\\varepsilon=f_{0,\\varepsilon}+\\varepsilon\\left(\\dot a_\\varepsilon-\\frac{\\eta(a_\\varepsilon,a_\\varepsilon)}{c^2}U_\\varepsilon\\right).\n$$\n\nUse the Euclidean norm of inertial components and its induced operator norm. In the SI interpretation $\\varepsilon$ is a time and $\\alpha_\\varepsilon=q/m$; all constants and field norms carry the corresponding units. Assume constants $A,M,P,C_0,C_1\\geq0$, independent of $\\varepsilon$, satisfy on $I$\n\n$$\n|\\alpha_\\varepsilon|\\leq A,\\quad \\|F\\|_{\\rm op}\\leq M,\\quad\n\\|\\Pi_{U_\\varepsilon}\\|_{\\rm op}\\leq P,\\quad\n\\|\\delta_\\varepsilon\\|\\leq C_0\\varepsilon,\\quad\n\\|\\dot\\delta_\\varepsilon\\|\\leq C_1\\varepsilon.\n$$\n\nThen throughout $I$,\n\n$$\n\\left\\|a_\\varepsilon-\\left(f_{0,\\varepsilon}+\\varepsilon\\Pi_{U_\\varepsilon}B_\\varepsilon\\right)\\right\\|\n\\leq P\\left(C_1+AM C_0\\right)\\varepsilon^2.\n$$\n\nThe estimate is conditional on the displayed common-interval and uniform bounds. It does not establish that regular LAD solutions satisfying them exist, nor does it imply global-in-time convergence.",
        "deps": [
          "lem-rpm-ll-reduced-ode",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-rpm-ll-reduced-ode": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Landau and Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, 4th revised English edition",
              "url": "https://ru.djvu.online/file/ADnW1S0k8Ttos",
              "locator": "Complete text inspected; §76, printed pp. 226–229. This source motivates reduction of order; the uniform conditional estimate below is proved directly."
            },
            {
              "title": "Sen Zhang, Pre-acceleration from Landau–Lifshitz Series, arXiv:1303.7120",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1303.7120",
              "locator": "Full HTML text inspected; §2, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.6). The source discusses perturbative order reduction and does not supply the uniform family bounds assumed here."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "physical_scope": "Conditional uniform residual estimate for regular flat LAD curve families already satisfying stated small-defect and bounded-coefficient hypotheses. It proves neither existence of such families nor global convergence of LAD trajectories to LL trajectories."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-rpm-lad-effective-equation",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Flat Lorentz–Abraham–Dirac effective equation",
        "statement": "Restrict [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]] to its flat affine Minkowski branch, with signature $(-,+,+,+)$, proper time $\\tau$ in seconds, future four-velocity $U=dx/d\\tau$ satisfying $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$, constant primitive $m>0$ in kilograms and nonzero charge $q\\in\\mathbb R$ in coulombs. Let the prescribed external electromagnetic two-form $F$ be $C^2$ on an open neighborhood of the worldline; use the component convention $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]. Let $a:=dU/d\\tau$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha:=q/m,\\qquad\n\\tau_e:=\\frac{q^2}{6\\pi\\epsilon_0mc^3}>0,\\qquad\nf_0^a:=\\alpha F^a{}_bU^b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ follows [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Adopt the flat LAD effective equation\n\n$$\na^a=f_0^a+\\tau_e\\left(\\frac{da^a}{d\\tau}-\\frac{\\eta_{bc}a^ba^c}{c^2}U^a\\right).\n$$\n\nThe derivative is the ordinary derivative in an inertial affine chart. Its dimensions are consistent: $a$ and $f_0$ are accelerations, the parenthesized term has acceleration per second, and $\\tau_e$ is a time. This is an adopted effective model equation for sufficiently regular curves (at least $C^3$); it is not asserted to be an exact law of nature, a point-source Maxwell construction, or a globally well-posed dynamics. In particular, no existence, uniqueness, stability, or empirical claim is part of this postulate.\n\nUse of the equation as a reduced physical approximation carries the source qualification that radiation reaction be small relative to the applied Lorentz force in the particle's instantaneous rest frame (the inertial frame momentarily comoving with $U$). This postulate neither asserts that condition for arbitrary $F$ nor supplies a numerical threshold; the qualification is not used to deduce the local ODE results or the force-free counterexample.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
          "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
          "def-em-si-conventions"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Landau and Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, 4th revised English edition",
              "url": "https://ru.djvu.online/file/ADnW1S0k8Ttos",
              "locator": "Complete text inspected; §§75–76, printed pp. 222–229. Gaussian-unit formulas are not copied as SI coefficients."
            },
            {
              "title": "Sen Zhang, Pre-acceleration from Landau–Lifshitz Series, arXiv:1303.7120",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1303.7120",
              "locator": "Full HTML text inspected; §2, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.6), and Appendix A, Eqs. (A.1)–(A.4), discussing the LAD equation and its reduced-order use."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "physical_scope": "Adopted flat-space point-particle effective equation in a prescribed external field. It is a model postulate, not a derivation from a coupled Maxwell point source, finite-body limit, or observation; it asserts no solution existence or global accuracy."
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-rpm-self-force-status",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Self-force status and point-particle limits",
        "statement": "The present pair prescribes an external field and adopts the flat LAD equation as a separate effective model. It does not construct a coupled Maxwell field sourced by a point charge or evaluate a self-field on the worldline. [[def-rpm-body-moments]] defines moments for a smooth finite body (and a local curved moment convention); its definitions do not assert a point limit or a self-force law.\n\nThe point-charge field is singular on the worldline, as reviewed by Poisson, Pound, and Vega; a self-force treatment therefore requires a specified regularization or extended-body limiting argument. Gralla, Harte, and Wald prove results for a particular one-parameter family of exact flat Maxwell–matter solutions in which body size, charge, and mass scale toward zero while the charge-to-mass ratio has a limit. Their leading motion is Lorentz-force motion and their first-order description includes self-force and multipole corrections. Their separate reduced-order discussion is qualified to regimes with negligible dipole and spin contributions. These results do not establish an unrestricted fixed-charge point construction, arbitrary-duration global accuracy, or the physical truth of the separately adopted postulate [[post-rpm-lad-effective-equation]].",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-body-moments",
          "post-rpm-lad-effective-equation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-body-moments": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-rpm-lad-effective-equation": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Eric Poisson, Adam Pound, and Ian Vega, The Motion of Point Particles in Curved Spacetime, Living Reviews in Relativity 14 (2011)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1102.0529v3",
              "locator": "Full text inspected; §1.2 and §22.1 on the singular point-charge field and the regular-field/tail formulation."
            },
            {
              "title": "Samuel E. Gralla, Abraham I. Harte, and Robert M. Wald, A Rigorous Derivation of Electromagnetic Self-force, Physical Review D 80, 024031 (2009)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0905.2391",
              "locator": "Complete 52-page paper inspected; §§I–III, V–VI, including the one-parameter small-body family, first-order corrections, and the authors’ qualified reduced-order discussion."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "physical_scope": "Scope remark separating prescribed-field LL/LAD effective equations, finite-body moments and literature results for a specific shrinking-body family. It supplies no point-charge self-field, universal fixed-charge point limit, or quantitative global accuracy claim."
      }
    ]
  },
